<Base
  title="Legal & Disclaimers"
  description="The disclaimers, disclosures, and fine print for this site — written so an actual human can read them."
>
  <span class="pill">The fine print, legible</span>
  <h1>Legal &amp; disclaimers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Congratulations — you are one of the eleven people in internet history to open a
    legal page on purpose. As a reward, this one is written to be read.
    The jokes are free; the disclaimers are binding. That's what she said.
  </p>

  <h2>Opinion and commentary</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      This is a commentary site. The show argues. On purpose. For hours. That is the
      product, and frankly it took years to perfect.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unless something is explicitly presented as a sourced fact — with a citation, the
      way the <a href="/statistics">statistics page</a> does it — statements on this site
      and on the show are <strong>opinion, analysis, rhetorical hyperbole, or satire</strong>.
      "This policy is insane" is a viewpoint, not a psychiatric diagnosis. Pearl is not
      licensed to diagnose policies, and no policy has ever shown up to its appointment anyway.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Guests, debate opponents, and commenters speak for themselves. Hosting a debate is
      not endorsing both sides of it — agreeing with both sides of a two-hour argument
      would technically be a medical condition, and see the section below: we don't
      diagnose those either.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Not legal advice</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      Nothing here is legal advice. Reading this site does not create an
      attorney-client relationship, no matter how many episodes you've watched.
      Binge-watching is a lifestyle, not a retainer agreement.
    </p>
    <p>
      Family court is jurisdiction-specific, deadline-driven, and unforgiving of
      guesswork — "but the podcast said" has a 0% success rate in front of a judge,
      and we intend to keep it that way. If you are in a custody or divorce case,
      talk to a licensed attorney in your state. The
      <a href="/resources">resources page</a> lists places to find one, including free
      legal aid directories.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Not financial or tax advice</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      Nothing here is investment, financial, or tax advice. Contributions to The
      Audacity Network go to <strong>JustPearlyThings, LLC</strong> — a company, not a
      charity — so <strong>do not deduct them on your taxes</strong> unless the checkout
      page one day identifies a qualified 501(c)(3) and hands you a receipt. The IRS
      has audited people for less, agrees with nobody, and has never once laughed.
      We checked. It's in the data.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Full details on where money goes: <a href="/transparency">transparency page</a>.
      Yes, we actually made one of those. Voluntarily. We're as surprised as you are.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Not medical or mental-health advice</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      This site discusses heavy topics, including suicide statistics. Discussing data
      is not treatment, and a podcast is not a clinician.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>If you or someone you know is struggling:</strong> in the US, call or text
      <strong>988</strong> (Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline), any hour, any day. That one
      is not a joke, and it never will be.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Accuracy and corrections</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      Published statistics link to primary sources and name the exact table, year, and
      limitation. If we got a number wrong, we would rather fix it than defend it —
      being corrected once is cheaper than being wrong forever, a principle we warmly
      recommend to everyone we debate.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Spotted an error? Email
      <a href="mailto:production@theaudacitynetwork.com">production@theaudacitynetwork.com</a>
      with the page and the source that contradicts it. Bring receipts — this is the
      one inbox on the internet where "well, actually" is genuinely welcome.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Third-party links</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      This site links out — to YouTube, ticketing pages, government datasets, other
      creators. We do not control those sites, their prices, their uptime, or their
      comment sections. Nobody controls comment sections. Comment sections are the
      one truly ungoverned territory left on Earth, and legally we must respect that.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      A link is a pointer, not a partnership, and outbound links are not paid
      placements unless a page says so explicitly. If we ever get sponsored, you will
      not have to guess — sponsors historically insist on being noticed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Copyright and fair use</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      Original content on this site belongs to JustPearlyThings, LLC. Clips of
      third-party material used in commentary rely on fair use — criticism, comment,
      and news reporting under 17 U.S.C. § 107. Quoting a claim in order to argue with
      it is the oldest genre in human history; Socrates did it professionally and
      somehow never got a copyright strike.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Rights holder with a concern? Email
      <a href="mailto:production@theaudacitynetwork.com">production@theaudacitynetwork.com</a>
      with the specific URL and the material at issue. Actual notices get actual
      answers; interpretive ones get read twice and then an actual answer anyway.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      This is a static site. No login, no tracking pixels, no ad network, and no cookie
      banner — because there are no cookies. We know that's disorienting. Take a moment.
      You are on a website that is not following you around the internet afterward,
      which in 2026 practically qualifies as performance art.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      We could not sell your data if we wanted to — we do not have any. Embedded players
      (like YouTube) and linked services set their own cookies under their own policies
      once you interact with them. That part of the internet is between you and Google's
      legal department, and we wish you both well.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="notice" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
    <strong>The unfunny footnote.</strong> This page is a plain-language summary of the
    site's disclaimers, not a substitute for formal Terms of Service or a Privacy Policy
    reviewed by a licensed attorney. Before launch, Pearl's counsel should review this
    page and the paid platform's <a href="https://www.theaudacitynetwork.com/terms" rel="noopener">existing terms</a>
    to make sure everything lines up. Lawyers, when you find the part you want to
    rewrite: yes, we know. We saved it for you.
  </p>
</Base>
